  "llm_analysis": {
    "hotspots": "Foundation model scaling & alignment (LLMs/VLMs), Agentic AI & multi-agent systems (planning, tool use, world modeling), Diffusion/generative models for high-fidelity synthesis (video, 3D, scientific data), Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) & long-context processing, AI for Scientific Discovery (simulation, physics-informed ML), Trustworthy AI (verification, robustness, safety, formal proofs).",
    "trends": "Shift from static models to stateful, interactive agents operating in environments (\"world models\"), Convergence of language, vision, and action (embodied AI / spatial intelligence), Rise of synthetic data & self-play for post-training alignment, Increased focus on inference-time compute scaling (test-time training, search, verifiers), Hardware-aware model design & efficient training/inference pipelines, Formal verification & mechanistic interpretability gaining traction.",
    "future_directions": "Autonomous agents with long-horizon reasoning & continual learning in open worlds, Unified world models integrating physics, semantics, and geometry for simulation & planning, Scaling laws for agentic systems & inference compute, Provably safe & aligned foundation models via formal methods, Personalized & private on-device foundation models, AI-driven scientific method automation (hypothesis -> simulation -> validation).",
    "analysis_summary": "The field is rapidly consolidating around foundation models as universal backbones, extended into agentic systems that perceive, reason, and act in complex environments. Core tensions drive research: scaling vs. efficiency, autonomy vs. control/verification, generality vs. specialization. The integration of language, vision, physics, and action (spatial/temporal reasoning) defines the next frontier, with rigorous evaluation, synthetic data, and formal guarantees becoming critical for real-world deployment."
